.# Abstract

This memo defines Taistamp, an HTTP-accessible service
that returns a server's current time as a TAI64N label,
optionally signed with Ed25519. The service is reached
at the well-known URI `/.well-known/taistamp` and provides
authenticated time readings without an NTP stack when
an authorized verification key is available. Verification
keys are obtained through authenticated DNS or independently
provisioned client configuration. A client without a usable
wall clock needs a key-authentication and transport route
that does not depend on that clock; configured verification
keys provide such a key-authentication route. DNSSEC and
certificate validation retain their time prerequisites.
The signing scheme uses length-prefixed, domain-separated
framing and binds the response to a client-supplied nonce.
DNS-published keys use a DKIM-inspired selector layout.

{mainmatter}

# Introduction

Many small clients need an accurate wall-clock reading
to validate certificates, evaluate signature freshness,
or stamp local events. Two common sources fall short:

- An NTP client is intrusive on constrained or sandboxed
  runtimes and exposes the device to a separate trust
  domain.
- TLS does not provide an explicit authenticated time
  value to the client; certificate validity checks
  depend on the client's existing clock rather than
  establishing a trustworthy time reading.

Taistamp addresses these by serving the current time as
a 25-byte TAI64N [@!TAI64] label over HTTP, with an
optional Ed25519 [@!RFC8032] signature bound to a
client-supplied nonce. Verification keys can be obtained
through authenticated DNS TXT records under a DKIM-style
[@?RFC6376] selector, or provisioned independently for a
service-domain and selector. DNS publication permits key
rotation without reconfiguring each client. Independently
configured keys require their own trusted update process.

A nonce removes the need for a wall clock when binding a
signed response to a request; it does not establish which
key the client should trust. Local DNSSEC validation needs
an independently justified time estimate sufficient for
its signature-validity checks. Certificate-based transport
authentication has its own validity checks. Clockless
bootstrap therefore requires a route whose authentication
prerequisites are already met, such as a configured
verification key over an explicitly permitted plaintext
HTTP connection. Without such a route, the client cannot
obtain an authenticated Level 2 result merely by querying
Taistamp. See (#clockless-bootstrap-and-key-authentication).

The protocol is deliberately small. A compliant
implementation fits in a single HTTP request handler, has no
state beyond its signing key, and is independent of any
specific HTTP framework or runtime.

# Design Rationale

## Why HTTP

HTTP is available everywhere a network connection is.
Browsers, serverless runtimes (Cloudflare Workers, AWS
Lambda, Deno Deploy), edge nodes, and CDN origins can
all serve and consume HTTP without additional
infrastructure. Port 443 (or 80) is open where
UDP 123 (NTP), TCP or UDP 5319 (Roughtime
[@?RFC10049]), and UDP 4014
(TAICLOCK [@?TAICLOCK]) are commonly firewalled.
A compliant Taistamp server requires nothing beyond a
single request handler; a compliant client requires
nothing beyond an HTTP library, both of which exist in
every language and runtime in common use today.

Taistamp works over plain HTTP. The Ed25519 signature
provides authentication and integrity at the
application layer, independent of the transport: any
in-transit modification breaks the signature, and a
client-supplied nonce prevents replay. These guarantees
apply only to responses bearing a valid `TAI-Signature`
matching a fresh request `TAI-Nonce`, verified with a key
already authorized for the service-domain. A network
attacker unable to sign with an authorized key can still
drop or delay responses. A nonce does not bound delay;
clients MUST account for that uncertainty when using the
reading, as described in (#threat-model).

TLS [@?RFC9846] is RECOMMENDED. It adds transport
confidentiality (hiding that a client is querying for
time and from which server) and an additional layer of
integrity. Where used, the two layers are
complementary: TLS protects the channel, the Ed25519
signature protects the claim independently of which
certificate authority issued the server certificate.

The server is stateless beyond its signing key. This
is a deliberate design goal: a stateless handler can
be deployed as a CDN edge function, a serverless
worker, or behind a load balancer with no session
affinity or shared state.

Taistamp is transported over TCP-based HTTP, which
rules out UDP-style spoofed-source amplification.
The protocol-mandated fields of a signed response
contribute at most approximately 530 bytes at maximum
field lengths, excluding transport framing and any
headers added by intermediaries — the response body
is a fixed 25 bytes, and the protocol-defined headers
(`TAI-Nonce` echo, `TAI-Signature`, `TAI-Key-Selector`,
`TAI-Leap-Seconds`) account for the remainder — an
attacker cannot induce a response meaningfully larger
than the request, so Taistamp is not usable as an
amplification vector.

## Why DNS-Based Key Discovery

Roughtime [@?RFC10049] addresses the same authenticated
time problem but distributes trust differently: clients
ship with a configured list of trusted server public keys.
Key rotation therefore requires a client update or a
coordinated key-distribution mechanism outside the
protocol.

Taistamp supports DNS TXT publication under a DKIM-inspired
[@?RFC6376] selector scheme. For clients using DNS-based
key discovery, operators rotate a key by publishing a new
TXT record and reconfiguring the server, subject to the
cache and authentication requirements in (#key-rotation)
and (#dns-trust). DNSSEC [@!RFC4033] authenticates the
key binding when its validation prerequisites are met.

This operational benefit does not make DNSSEC independent
of time. A configured verification key is an alternative
for clients that cannot yet authenticate DNS key data.
This uses the same kind of pre-established service-key
trust as the configured-key model described above; it
is not automatically refreshed or revoked through DNS.
The application must arrange trusted provisioning and
key updates as described in
(#independently-configured-verification-keys).

This approach deliberately trades the ecosystem-level
misbehaviour detection that Roughtime's multi-server
cross-checking provides for simpler, decentralised
deployability:
any operator with a domain name and an HTTP endpoint can
run a Taistamp server without registering with a central
authority.

## Why TAI64N

POSIX time and UTC both admit leap seconds, making a
given second ambiguous: a timestamp of 23:59:60 is
legal in UTC but meaningless in most system clocks,
which either smear the leap or repeat the last second.
For applications that check certificate validity windows
or evaluate signature freshness, an ambiguous second is
a correctness hazard.

TAI [@!TAI64] is strictly monotonic. TAI64N encodes TAI
seconds and nanoseconds in a fixed 25-byte ASCII label
with no ambiguity at any leap-second boundary. The
`TAI-Leap-Seconds` header carries the offset as informational
guidance; clients requiring UTC equivalence MUST source the offset
from a trusted table rather than from the server
(see (#leap-second-reporting)).

## Why the External Representation

TAICLOCK [@?TAICLOCK] encodes timestamps as 16 bytes of
binary TAI64NA (8 bytes of seconds, 4 of nanoseconds,
4 of attoseconds). Taistamp instead uses the TAI64N
external representation [@!TAI64]: the ASCII character
`@` followed by 16 lowercase hexadecimal digits for
the seconds field and 8 for the nanoseconds field,
totalling 25 bytes.

The 9-byte difference over TAICLOCK's binary encoding
arises from three sources: the `@` leader (+1 byte),
hex-expanding the 8-byte seconds field to 16 ASCII
digits (+8 bytes), and hex-expanding the 4-byte
nanoseconds field to 8 ASCII digits (+4 bytes), offset
by dropping the 4-byte attosecond field that TAI64NA
carries and TAI64N omits (−4 bytes).

The choice is driven by the HTTP context. A hex-encoded
ASCII label is trivially inspectable with `curl`, safe
to log as plain text, and requires no additional
encoding in an HTTP response body. The 9-byte overhead
over binary is negligible in an HTTP exchange.
Attoseconds are omitted because no deployed clock
provides sub-nanosecond resolution.

## Why Ed25519

Ed25519 [@!RFC8032] was chosen for three reasons.
First, verification is fast and keys and signatures are
compact: a public key is 32 bytes and a signature is 64
bytes, keeping the signed response small and the
verification cost negligible on constrained clients.
Second, signing is deterministic — no per-signature
random nonce is required, which removes an entire class
of implementation hazard present in ECDSA. Third,
Ed25519 has no negotiable parameters; there is nothing
to downgrade.

## Why Nonce-Based Anti-Replay

A client-side freshness test that rejects a response older
than a configured interval requires an approximately
correct wall clock. A fresh, unpredictable client nonce
avoids that prerequisite for binding a signed response to
a particular request: the server signs the nonce together
with the returned label, so an earlier response to a
different nonce cannot satisfy the new request.

This removes a clock dependency from request freshness,
not from verification-key authentication. The client must
still establish an authorized service-domain-to-key
binding by a route whose own prerequisites are met.
A matching nonce alone does not authenticate the responder
or its claimed time, and a nonce does not limit how long
an attacker can delay a response. See
(#clockless-bootstrap-and-key-authentication).

## Precision Expectations

The TAI64N format encodes nanoseconds, but server
implementations are commonly limited to millisecond
resolution. Runtimes such as Cloudflare Workers
deliberately freeze the clock at the last I/O boundary
as a Spectre mitigation; no sub-millisecond source is
available. Clients SHOULD NOT assume that the
nanosecond field carries sub-millisecond information;
in practice the last six decimal digits of the
nanosecond value are often zero.

## Non-Goals

Taistamp does not synchronise or discipline a local
clock; callers MUST treat the returned label as a
single measurement subject to network round-trip
uncertainty. It does not protect against a server
operator who deliberately signs an incorrect time: the
signature attests to what the server claimed, not to
the accuracy of its clock. It does not provide
confidentiality; TLS [@?RFC9846] SHOULD be used when
confidentiality of the query or response is required.
It is not a document timestamping service in the sense
of RFC 3161 [@?RFC3161]: it timestamps moments,
not artefacts.

# Relation to Other Protocols

**TAICLOCK [@?TAICLOCK]** is D. J. Bernstein's UDP-based
TAI64N time service (IANA port 4014). A client sends a
packet between 20 and 256 bytes whose first four bytes
are the ASCII string `ctai`; the server echoes the
packet with the first byte replaced by `s` and bytes
4–19 replaced with the current time in TAI64NA format.
TAICLOCK is the direct conceptual predecessor of
Taistamp: both serve TAI64N timestamps on demand.
Taistamp differs in using HTTP (deployable over existing
HTTPS infrastructure, no dedicated daemon), adding
optional Ed25519 signatures,
and publishing keys via DNS rather than relying on the
transport for integrity.

**RFC 5905 (NTP) [@?RFC5905]** synchronises a local
clock continuously over UDP, adjusting for network
delay across multiple exchanges. Taistamp makes no
attempt to discipline a local clock; it provides a
single authenticated reading that the caller treats as
a measurement subject to one round-trip time.

**RFC 8915 (NTS) [@?RFC8915]** adds cryptographic
authentication to NTP using TLS for key exchange and
AEAD for packet authentication. NTS inherits NTP's
requirement for a dedicated daemon and UDP port 123.
Taistamp targets runtimes where neither is available.

**RFC 3161 (TSP) [@?RFC3161]** is a document
timestamping protocol: a client submits a hash of a
document and receives a signed token attesting that the
document existed at a given time. TSP timestamps
artefacts; Taistamp timestamps moments. The two
protocols are complementary rather than overlapping.

**Roughtime [@?RFC10049]** is an authenticated time
protocol that also uses Ed25519 signatures and is
designed for constrained clients. It operates over UDP or TCP,
distributes trust via a configured list of server
public keys, and detects misbehaving servers by
comparing timestamps and accuracy radii across
multiple providers. Taistamp is simpler and more narrowly
scoped: a single HTTP endpoint, a choice of DNS-based
or independently configured verification keys, and no
ecosystem coordination requirement. Configured-key
operation shares the provisioning and rollover trade-off
of other protocols using pre-established service keys.

# Conventions and Definitions

The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL",
"SHALL NOT", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED",
"NOT RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this
document are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14
[@!RFC2119] [@!RFC8174] when, and only when, they
appear in all capitals, as shown here.

The RFC Editor's BCP 14 overview [@?BCP14] lists
its constituent RFCs.

The terms "TAI" and "TAI64N" are used as defined in
[@!TAI64].

The term "selector" is used in the DKIM
sense [@?RFC6376]: an opaque, service-domain-scoped
label that selects one verification key from possibly
many published under the same service-domain.

The term "client" refers to any HTTP agent that fetches
the Taistamp resource at `/.well-known/taistamp`.

The term "verifier" refers to a client that performs
signature verification on a Taistamp response.

A "service URI" is the HTTP or HTTPS URI selected by
an application before endpoint discovery. Its
"service-domain" is the DNS name in the URI's host
subcomponent [@!RFC3986], excluding any port number
and terminal root dot. Internationalized domain labels
MUST use their IDNA A-label representation [@!RFC5890].
For application cache keys, the ASCII service-domain
MUST be normalized to lowercase. This normalization
MUST NOT modify the selector octets in a signed payload.

The service-domain identifies the verification-key
namespace. It is distinct from a DNS discovery query
name, a connection endpoint, and the HTTP origin, which
also includes the scheme and port [@!RFC9110]. Throughout
this document, `<host>` in a key owner name denotes the
service-domain, not the host of a discovered endpoint.

DNS queries constructed by this protocol MUST use
absolute names and respect the DNS name-length limits
in [@!RFC1035]. A client MUST NOT append a local search
suffix or truncate a constructed name to make it fit.
If no valid key owner name can be constructed, the
client cannot complete DNS-based key verification.
An IP-literal service URI has no service-domain for
this procedure; a client MUST NOT use reverse DNS to
invent one. Access without DNS-based verification
remains possible. Independently configured verification
keys for IP-literal URIs are outside this specification.

A "usable wall clock" is an independently justified time
estimate adequate for the validity checks being performed.
It is distinct from a monotonic elapsed-time source. A
candidate Taistamp reading is not an independent time
estimate for authenticating its own verification key.

HTTP field values defined in this document use
Structured Field Values [@!RFC9651]. Type annotations
(Integer, Binary, Token) refer to the item types
defined in that specification.

# Resource

A Taistamp service is reached at the well-known
URI [@!RFC8615]:

~~~
/.well-known/taistamp
~~~

A request URI MUST be the exact path above. Query
strings, if any, are ignored by this version of the
protocol and MUST NOT change the response.

## Methods

A server MUST accept `GET` and `HEAD`. A server that
supports CORS (see (#cross-origin-resource-sharing))
MUST also accept `OPTIONS`; a server without CORS
support MAY accept `OPTIONS`. A server MUST respond
`405 Method Not Allowed` to any method it does not
accept. The `Allow` header on a 405 response, and
on a successful `OPTIONS` response, MUST enumerate
the methods the server accepts ([@!RFC9110],
Section 10.2.1): a server that accepts `OPTIONS`
emits `Allow: GET, HEAD, OPTIONS`; one that does
not emits `Allow: GET, HEAD`.

A `HEAD` response MUST NOT include a body and MUST
carry the same headers as the corresponding `GET`
response, with the exception that `TAI-Nonce`,
`TAI-Key-Selector`, and `TAI-Signature` MUST NOT
be present. Because the signed payload covers the
response body, a signature cannot be verified
without it; `HEAD` is therefore useful only as a
liveness or configuration probe. Note that this
makes `HEAD` responses non-equivalent to the
corresponding `GET` response in the sense of
[@!RFC9110] Section 9.3.2; carrying an unverifiable
signature on a bodyless response would be misleading.

`OPTIONS` responses MUST NOT carry any `TAI-*` response
header and MUST NOT be signed.

## Cross-Origin Resource Sharing

A Taistamp server intended for access from browser pages
SHOULD respond to `OPTIONS` requests with status `200`
and the following headers:

- `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or a site-specific
  origin policy)
- `Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, HEAD`
- `Access-Control-Allow-Headers: TAI-Nonce`
- `Access-Control-Expose-Headers: TAI-Leap-Seconds,
  TAI-Nonce, TAI-Key-Selector, TAI-Signature`
- `Access-Control-Max-Age: 600` (or a higher operator-
  chosen value)

A browser making a cross-origin request that includes the
`TAI-Nonce` header will send an `OPTIONS` preflight before
the actual `GET`; without the appropriate
`Access-Control-*` response headers the browser will block
the request. Non-browser clients are unaffected.

Servers SHOULD emit `Access-Control-Max-Age` with a
value of at least 600 seconds. Pre-flight responses
carry no per-request data and are safe to cache for
the lifetime of the deployed CORS configuration.
In the absence of this header, browser pre-flight
cache lifetimes diverge significantly across
implementations, which may cause a pre-flight
request to precede every cross-origin GET in
high-traffic deployments.

## Successful Response

A successful `GET` response has status `200 OK` and a
body consisting of exactly 25 ASCII bytes: the TAI64N
external representation of the server's current time,
formatted per [@!TAI64]:

~~~
'@' hex16(seconds-since-TAI-epoch) hex8(nanoseconds)
~~~

where `hex16` and `hex8` denote 16 and 8 lowercase
hexadecimal digits respectively. The leading `@` is
literal.

The following headers MUST be present:

| Header           | Value                                |
|------------------|--------------------------------------|
| `Content-Type`   | `application/tai64n`                 |
| `Content-Length` | `25`                                 |
| `Cache-Control`  | `no-store`                           |
| `TAI-Leap-Seconds` | sf-integer, e.g. `37`              |

`TAI-Leap-Seconds` carries the offset, in seconds,
between TAI and UTC at the moment of the response,
as a Structured Field Integer [@!RFC9651]. leap-seconds
emitted MUST be a non-negative integer in [0, 2^32−1];
sf-integer supports values up to ±10^15 but values outside
[0, 2^32−1] cannot be encoded in `leap-u32be`. A verifier
that receives a `TAI-Leap-Seconds` value outside this range
MUST treat the response as unsigned.
Its value is included in the signed payload as `leap-u32be`
(see (#framed-payload)). A server MUST emit it on
every successful response. It is a singleton field
(Section 5.5 of [@!RFC9110]) and MUST NOT appear
more than once in a response. A recipient that
encounters more than one instance MUST treat the field
as absent per Section 4.2 of [@!RFC9651];
the recipient then has no server-supplied TAI-to-UTC
offset for this response and MUST treat the response
as unsigned regardless of whether `TAI-Signature` is present.

## Nonce Echo

A client MAY send a request header `TAI-Nonce` carrying
an opaque byte string encoded as a Structured Field
Binary [@!RFC9651]. If present on a `GET` request, the server
MUST include a `TAI-Nonce` response field whose
`sf-binary` value encodes the same octet sequence
as the request field. `TAI-Nonce` is a
singleton field (Section 5.5 of [@!RFC9110]) and MUST
NOT appear more than once in a request or response. A
recipient that encounters more than one instance MUST
treat the field as absent per Section 4.2 of
[@!RFC9651]. A server that treats a request `TAI-Nonce`
as absent for this reason MUST NOT sign the response.
A server MAY instead respond with `400 Bad Request` when
a request contains duplicate `TAI-Nonce` fields.
A client that treats a response `TAI-Nonce` as absent
MUST treat the response as unsigned regardless of
whether `TAI-Signature` is present.

A server MUST NOT interpret the nonce. If a server
receives a `TAI-Nonce` that is a malformed sf-binary
it MUST treat the field as absent and MUST NOT sign
the response. The decoded `sf-binary` value MUST contain at least
7 octets and MUST NOT exceed 129 octets. When
serialized as `sf-binary`, these correspond exactly
to field values of 14 octets (: + 12 base64 +
padding + :) and 174 octets (: + 172 base64 + :)
respectively; implementations MAY reject on wire
length before decoding as an optimisation, but the
normative check is on decoded length.

The lower bound of 7 decoded octets provides
sufficient client-supplied entropy for the replay
defence in this section. The upper bound of 129
decoded octets caps the nonce's contribution to
overall response size; combined with the 25-byte body and
the other protocol-defined response headers, the
protocol-mandated fields contribute at most
approximately 530 bytes in total, excluding transport
framing and intermediary-added headers. Implementations
that require
larger nonces for compatibility with a higher-entropy
convention may negotiate a future extension; this
version of the protocol holds the bounds fixed for
interoperability.

A server that receives a `TAI-Nonce`
outside this range MUST NOT sign the response.
A zero-length or syntactically empty field value is below
the minimum and MUST be treated as absent.
Clients SHOULD choose nonces that are unpredictable to a
network observer when freshness matters; 16 random
bytes encoded as an sf-binary value are sufficient for
typical use.

When a client included a `TAI-Nonce` in the request,
the trust level of the response is determined as follows.
If the response carries no `TAI-Nonce` field, the response
is Plain (level 0) regardless of whether a signature is
present; a conformant server only signs responses that echo
a nonce (see (#signing)), so any signature in such a
response MUST be ignored. If the response carries a
`TAI-Nonce` whose decoded octets do not match the request
nonce, the response is Inconsistent (level -1) and MUST be
rejected regardless of signature state. If the response
carries a `TAI-Nonce` whose decoded octets match the request
nonce, the trust level depends on the key/signature state as
defined in (#trust-levels). The key/signature state is
Unresolvable when no authorized key can be obtained for
the well-formed selector under the client's preselected
key-discovery policy; a malformed selector has state
Malformed. Valid means that the signature verifies with
an authorized key, not merely a key received from the
network. These cases are summarised in the following table.

~~~
+----------------+---------+--------------+--------------+-------+
| Response nonce | Matches | Key/Sig      | Outcome      | Level |
+----------------+---------+--------------+--------------+-------+
| Absent         | N/A     | Any          | Plain        |  0    |
| Present        | No      | Any          | Inconsistent | -1    |
| Present        | Yes     | Absent       | Unique       |  1    |
| Present        | Yes     | Malformed    | Unique       |  1    |
| Present        | Yes     | Unresolvable | Unique       |  1    |
| Present        | Yes     | Invalid      | Inconsistent | -1    |
| Present        | Yes     | Valid        | Signed       |  2    |
+----------------+---------+--------------+--------------+-------+
~~~
Figure: Client trust level by response nonce and key/signature state.

A network attacker who strips the `TAI-Nonce` from the
outgoing request causes the server to respond at level 0.
A client that requires level 1 or above treats this as a
denial of service; a client that accepts level 0 receives
no freshness guarantee.

## Service Identity and Endpoint Aliases

Before processing discovery results or response fields,
a client MUST retain the service-domain selected by the
application. Endpoint selection MUST NOT change it.
A same-host HTTP-to-HTTPS upgrade permitted by the
client's HTTP transport policy may change the scheme
and effective port; the resulting origin MUST then be
preserved during endpoint selection.

Ordinary CNAME resolution [@!RFC1034] MAY be used to
host a Taistamp service at another provider. For example:

~~~
time.example.com. 300 IN CNAME c17.provider.example.
~~~

For a request to
`https://time.example.com/.well-known/taistamp`, this
alias does not change the service-domain or the initial
key query `<selector>._taistamp.time.example.com`.
A hostname CNAME affects that hostname, not only the
Taistamp resource path. It does not alias descendant
key names; see (#delegated-key-publication).

The HTTP `Host` or `:authority` MUST identify the
service URI authority after any permitted upgrade,
not the discovered connection destination. HTTPS
certificate verification MUST authenticate the
original service-domain according to [@!RFC9110] and
[@!RFC9525], rather than a CNAME or service-binding
target. TLS server-name signaling follows the applicable
HTTPS rules; HTTPS-record processing is described below.

An HTTP redirect is not signing-key delegation. Except
for same-host HTTP-to-HTTPS upgrades permitted by the
client's HTTP transport policy, a change of HTTP origin
MUST be authorized by the application as a new Taistamp
transaction with its own trust policy and a new nonce.
A client MUST NOT silently derive a different key
namespace from a redirect or the final response URL.
Any followed redirect MUST preserve the resource path
specified in (#resource).

## Endpoint Discovery Using HTTPS Resource Records

Clients MAY use the HTTP service binding defined in
Section 9 of [@!RFC9460], either directly or through an
HTTP stack that implements it. Such clients MUST use
HTTPS resource records, not generic SVCB resource
records, for the HTTP and HTTPS service URIs defined
here. This specification adds no Taistamp-specific
SVCB query, SRV discovery procedure, or URI scheme.

Query-name construction, CNAME and AliasMode processing,
ServiceMode compatibility, connection parameters,
endpoint ordering, DNS errors, and fallback MUST follow
[@!RFC9460], subject to the application's existing
security policy. This document adds no exception to
HTTP-to-HTTPS upgrade rules. In particular, the binding
port is not a signal to use plaintext HTTP.

For a default-port HTTPS origin the initial HTTPS query
name is its hostname. Non-default origin ports use the
port-prefixed form defined in Section 9.1 of
[@!RFC9460]; for example, `https://time.example.com:9443`
uses `_9443._https.time.example.com.`. These discovery
names are not verification-key namespaces.

After any permitted HTTP-to-HTTPS upgrade, the HTTPS
origin MUST remain unchanged by endpoint selection.
A ServiceMode `port` parameter changes only the
connection port, not the origin port in `Host` or
`:authority`. The TLS reference identity remains the
original service-domain. Server-name signaling MUST
follow Section 9.4 of [@!RFC9460], including when the
ClientHello is encrypted.

The following is an alternative to the hostname CNAME
configuration above. The A record provides a direct
endpoint for clients not using HTTPS records:

~~~
time.example.com. 300 IN A 192.0.2.20
time.example.com. 300 IN HTTPS 0 c17.provider.example.
c17.provider.example. 300 IN HTTPS 1 edge.provider.example. (
    alpn="h2,http/1.1" port=8443 )
edge.provider.example. 300 IN A 192.0.2.10
~~~

A client selecting that ServiceMode endpoint connects
to `edge.provider.example:8443`, but its origin remains
`https://time.example.com` and its HTTP authority remains
`time.example.com`. Both the direct HTTPS endpoint and
the alternative endpoint need a certificate valid for
`time.example.com` and suitable virtual-host routing.
The initial key lookup remains
`<selector>._taistamp.time.example.com`.

HTTPS records apply to an origin, not an individual
path. Operators needing Taistamp hosting separate from
their website SHOULD use a dedicated service-domain or
arrange server-side routing of the well-known resource.
HTTPS-record support is optional; operators wishing to
serve clients without it SHOULD retain a working direct
endpoint through ordinary address resolution.

A client implementing the TCP-based transport specified
in (#why-http) MUST restrict endpoint selection to
HTTP over TCP. Compatibility is evaluated with the
applicable default and advertised ALPN protocols under
[@!RFC9460]. This document does not add an HTTP/3 binding.

An application relying on its HTTP stack for discovery
MUST keep the service-domain separate from the stack's
connection destination. Endpoint selection, retries,
and fallback MUST NOT lower its configured minimum
acceptable Taistamp authentication level. Each new HTTP
attempt SHOULD use a fresh, unpredictable nonce.
Discovery caches and derived endpoint mappings MUST
respect the applicable DNS and validation lifetimes.

Direct plaintext HTTP remains supported subject to the
client's transport policy. This section neither defines
a plaintext service binding nor permits bypassing an
applicable HTTPS upgrade or certificate check to obtain
a timestamp. Time bootstrapping limitations are discussed
in (#delegation-security).

# Signing

A Taistamp service MAY be configured with an Ed25519
[@!RFC8032] signing key and a selector. When both are
configured, the server signs every response that
carries a `TAI-Nonce`.

A signed successful response carries two additional headers:

| Header              | Value                                  |
|---------------------|----------------------------------------|
| `TAI-Key-Selector`  | the selector, as an sf-token           |
| `TAI-Signature`     | the Ed25519 signature, as an sf-binary |

The selector MUST match the following ABNF production
[@!RFC5234]. The grammar is constrained to the subset
of DNS LDH labels ([@!RFC1035] Section 2.3.1) that are
also valid sf-tokens ([@!RFC9651]); the leading-ALPHA
requirement follows from sf-token, not from DNS alone,
because sf-token requires its first character to be
ALPHA or `*` ([@!RFC9651] Section 4.1.7), excluding
digit-leading labels that DNS would otherwise permit:

~~~
selector = ALPHA [ *61( ALPHA / DIGIT / "-" )
                   ( ALPHA / DIGIT ) ]
~~~

`TAI-Key-Selector` is a singleton field
(Section 5.5 of [@!RFC9110]) and MUST NOT appear more
than once in a response. A recipient that encounters
more than one instance MUST treat the field as absent
per Section 4.2 of [@!RFC9651]; the response MUST
then be treated as unsigned.

`TAI-Signature` is a singleton field (Section 5.5 of
[@!RFC9110]) and MUST NOT appear more than once in a
response. A recipient that encounters more than one
instance MUST treat the field as absent per Section
4.2 of [@!RFC9651]; the response MUST then be treated
as unsigned.

A response that carries `TAI-Signature` without
`TAI-Key-Selector` MUST be treated as unsigned; the
absent selector yields key/signature state Absent, as
if no signature were present.

## Framed Payload

The signed payload is the concatenation, in order, of
the following fields:

~~~
domain-tag    = "taistamp-v1" %x00
label-bytes   = 25 octets ; the response body
leap-u32be    = 4 octets  ; TAI-Leap-Seconds,
              ;   big-endian unsigned
selector-len  = 1 octet   ; length of selector-bytes, 1..63
selector-bytes = 1..63 octets ; the selector, ASCII
nonce-bytes   = octets    ; decoded sf-binary octets
              ;   of the TAI-Nonce field value

payload = domain-tag label-bytes leap-u32be
          selector-len selector-bytes nonce-bytes
~~~

nonce-bytes is the octet sequence obtained by
parsing the `TAI-Nonce` field value as an
`sf-binary` item per [@!RFC9651] and decoding
the base64 value. The textual representation
of the field is not part of the signed input;
only the decoded bytes are signed.

The trailing NUL on `domain-tag` is significant: it
prevents extension of the tag into adjacent bytes by
an attacker who controls the protocol version string.

The selector is length-prefixed by a single byte. A
downgrade attacker who rewrites `TAI-Key-Selector` on
the wire cannot match an existing signature without
also matching its length, as the signature is bound
to the exact payload including the length byte;
forging a valid signature for a different selector
length is computationally infeasible under the
existential unforgeability of Ed25519.

The nonce occupies the tail of the payload. Its length
is therefore self-delimiting: a verifier computes
`nonce-bytes` from the decoded `TAI-Nonce` sf-binary
value and appends it as the final payload component,
with no explicit length field required.

## When to Sign

A server MUST sign a response if and only if all of
the following hold:

- The server is configured with a key and selector.
- The request carried a `TAI-Nonce` header.
- The response is a 200 to `GET`.

A request without a nonce MAY receive an unsigned
response even when the server is configured to sign.
This allows lightweight unsigned probes to coexist with
authenticated reads, though the response itself MUST
remain `Cache-Control: no-store` because the label is
time-sensitive.

## Intermediary Handling

An intermediary (proxy or gateway) that forwards a
Taistamp request or response MUST forward the
`TAI-Nonce`, `TAI-Leap-Seconds`, `TAI-Key-Selector`,
and `TAI-Signature` fields intact and MUST NOT modify
or remove them. Stripping or rewriting `TAI-Nonce` on
a request prevents the server from signing the response.
Stripping or rewriting any of the response fields
prevents the client from verifying the signature.

These requirements apply to Taistamp-aware intermediaries.
General-purpose proxies that do not recognise `TAI-*` fields
may strip or modify them without violating their own
specifications. Operators who require signed responses SHOULD
avoid placing non-transparent intermediaries between client and
server, or verify that any intermediaries are configured to
forward these fields intact.

# Key Publication

This section describes DNS publication and independent
configuration of verification keys for services configured
with a signing key and selector. A service intended for
DNS-based discovery MUST publish its verification key as
a DNS TXT record. A configured-key-only deployment need
not publish that record.

For DNS publication, the owner name is

~~~
<selector> "._taistamp." <host>
~~~

where `<host>` is the service-domain established from
the application-selected service URI before discovery
(see (#conventions-and-definitions)), and `<selector>`
is the value the server emits in `TAI-Key-Selector`.
A CNAME target, HTTPS AliasMode target, ServiceMode
target, or final response URL MUST NOT replace `<host>`.
DNS aliases encountered while resolving this key owner
are handled separately in (#delegated-key-publication).

Neither the URI scheme nor its port is part of the key
owner name. Services using the same service-domain at
different schemes or ports therefore share this key
namespace; they do not gain separate signing identities
merely by using different connection endpoints.

The TXT record value is a single string, of length at
most 255 octets, formatted as a tag-value list in the
DKIM/DMARC style:

~~~
v=tai1; k=ed25519; p=<base64-of-32-raw-pubkey-bytes>
~~~

| Tag | Value |
|-----|-------|
| `v` | Protocol version. `tai1` for the framing in this document. |
| `k` | Key algorithm. `ed25519` is the only algorithm currently defined. |
| `p` | Public key, standard base64 of the 32 raw Ed25519 public key bytes. |


Parsing is done as specified in Section 3.2 of [@!RFC6376].
A verifier MUST treat unknown tags as informational.
A verifier MUST treat an unknown `v` or `k` as an
unrecoverable failure for that selector and MUST NOT
fall back silently.

## Independently Configured Verification Keys

A client MAY use independently configured Ed25519 public
keys instead of DNS-based key discovery. The configuration
MUST bind each authorized 32-octet public key to the retained
service-domain and a well-formed selector. Configured
selector matching MUST be exact octet matching; the
selector's original octets remain part of the signed
payload. Multiple selectors MAY be configured for one
service-domain, for example to prepare for key rollover.
This specification defines no configuration-file format.

The application MUST select the key-discovery mode for
the service-domain before making the request. In configured-key
mode, the verifier MUST select the key only from that
configuration and MUST NOT perform DNS key discovery for
the transaction. Routing lookups for connection establishment
remain subject to the application's transport policy.
The received selector identifies an entry in the configured
set; it does not authorize a new key or a different domain.

The configuration MUST come from an independently trusted
source. Learning a key from the unauthenticated response
or its DNS path and then storing it is not independent
provisioning. The client MUST protect the configuration
against unauthorized modification and rollback to withdrawn
keys. The application MUST specify how its authorized key
set is installed, updated, and revoked; this document does
not define an on-wire key-update protocol.

An unknown selector, a verification failure, or an expired
local authorization MUST NOT cause automatic fallback to
DNS key discovery or acceptance below the application's
required authentication level. Modes can be changed only
through an independently authorized application decision,
not by a response or network failure.

Configured keys have no protocol-defined time validity
fields. Where a deployment adds time-based authorization
conditions, it MUST satisfy them independently before
using the key for clockless bootstrap; it MUST NOT use
the candidate reading to make its own key eligible.

DNS TTLs and removal of DNS records do not expire or revoke
configured authorization. A client holding a compromised
configured key remains exposed until its trusted
configuration withdraws that key. Automatic rollover for
DNS clients does not update configured-key clients; a
provider serving both modes MUST plan both lifecycles.

## Key Rotation

DNS-published key rotation follows a publish-switch-retire
sequence. This sequence does not update
independently configured keys; see
(#independently-configured-verification-keys).

Operators SHOULD choose a DNS TTL for the TXT
record that balances query load against key rotation
speed. A TTL between 3600 seconds (1 hour) and
86400 seconds (24 hours) is RECOMMENDED for stable
keys. When an operator plans to rotate a key, they
SHOULD lower the TTL of the existing TXT record
well in advance of the rotation to ensure that
verifier caches clear quickly once the new key
is deployed.

**Publish.** The operator publishes a new TXT record
under a new selector, or a safely reusable inactive
selector as described in (#delegated-key-publication).
Before reconfiguring the server, the operator MUST
allow cached earlier key bindings and negative answers
to expire. Relevant lifetimes include the TXT and any
alias TTLs, and negative-cache lifetimes [@!RFC2308].
Lowering a TTL does not shorten the lifetime of entries
already cached with an earlier, longer TTL.

**Switch.** The server is reconfigured to sign
responses with the new key and selector.

**Retire.** The old TXT record SHOULD remain in DNS
for at least one DNS TTL interval after the switch,
to allow verifiers that cached the old record to
complete any in-flight verification. The old TXT
record MAY then be removed.

### Verifier Key Caching

A verifier MAY cache the parsed public key, but each
application cache entry MUST be scoped by both the
service-domain and selector. A cache indexed only by
selector or provider hostname is not sufficient. DNS
case normalization MUST NOT change the selector octets
used when reconstructing the signed payload.

A cached binding MUST NOT outlive the remaining DNS
TTL returned by the resolver. For an aliased lookup,
its lifetime MUST NOT exceed the shortest remaining
lifetime of the alias records and terminal TXT RRset.
Any earlier expiry required by DNSSEC validation also
applies [@!RFC4035]. A client MAY rely on a resolver
that supplies these effective limits. If the necessary
limits are unavailable, it MUST NOT cache the derived
binding independently of that resolver.

Verifiers MUST NOT replace DNS- and validation-derived
limits with a hardcoded lifetime. Earlier cache eviction
is permitted, but expired entries MUST NOT be used.
A binding MAY be refreshed by resolving the original
key owner again under the same trust policy. If a
signature fails against a cached key, the verifier
SHOULD re-resolve that owner once before treating the
signature as invalid, subject to the lookup limits in
(#selector-based-dns-amplification).

### Key Revocation

If a DNS-published signing key is compromised, the
service operator MUST withdraw its DNS authorization
without a normal retirement period. This can remove the key TXT record
or remove or replace a selector CNAME that authorizes
it. The operator MUST account for every remaining key
publication path that authorizes the compromised key
for its service-domain. No separate out-of-band
revocation channel is specified.

For a delegated key zone, changes to the delegation
require coordinated handling of NS and DS records,
where applicable. Removing an NS record alone MUST NOT
be assumed to revoke cached or otherwise still-trusted
keys immediately.

A verifier can continue accepting signatures made with
the key while its cached authorization remains valid
under (#verifier-key-caching). A short TTL reduces
ordinary cache retention, but is not a complete bound
on revocation exposure: still-valid signed DNS records
can be replayed, as discussed in (#key-revocation-window).
Operators SHOULD consider alias and TXT TTLs, as well
as DNSSEC signature lifetimes, when planning revocation.

## Delegated Key Publication

A key owner of the form
`<selector>._taistamp.<service-domain>` MAY be a CNAME.
Verifiers MUST support ordinary CNAME resolution
[@!RFC1034] for this TXT lookup, directly or through
their resolver. They MUST detect alias loops and bound
alias traversal and aggregate DNS work. Authentication
of the resulting binding follows (#dns-trust) and
(#delegation-security); caching follows
(#verifier-key-caching).

For example, a customer can publish these aliases while
the provider publishes the terminal TXT records:

~~~
s1._taistamp.time.example.com. 300 IN CNAME (
    s1.c17.keys.provider.example. )
s2._taistamp.time.example.com. 300 IN CNAME (
    s2.c17.keys.provider.example. )
~~~

Each terminal TXT record uses the format in
(#key-publication). The initial query still uses the
customer's service-domain. Neither a hostname CNAME
nor a CNAME at `_taistamp.<service-domain>` aliases
selector names below it. Clients MUST NOT interpret
such a record as an application-specific suffix-rewriting
rule. A CNAME also cannot coexist with ordinary TXT
or NS data at its own owner [@!RFC2181].

An operator MAY instead delegate the entire
`_taistamp.<service-domain>` child zone with NS records
[@!RFC1034] [@!RFC2181]. For example, the parent zone
can publish:

~~~
_taistamp.time.example.com. 300 IN NS ns1.provider.example.
_taistamp.time.example.com. 300 IN NS ns2.provider.example.
~~~

The provider then operates an ordinary child zone,
including its SOA, authoritative NS, and key TXT records.
To maintain a DNSSEC authentication chain from the
parent, the child MUST be signed and the parent MUST
publish the appropriate DS record [@!RFC4035]. A
separately configured trust anchor is an alternative
trust arrangement, not a substitute parent DS record.

NS delegation allows autonomous selector creation.
Alternatively, a provider MAY rotate through a fixed
pool of preconfigured selector CNAMEs. Before reusing
an inactive selector, it MUST let earlier key bindings
and any negative-cache entries expire and preserve the
publish-switch-retire ordering in (#key-rotation).
This permits repeated rotations without requiring a
customer DNS edit for every new key.

# Trust Levels

A Taistamp response is assigned one of four trust levels,
combining the nonce echo result and the authorized
key/signature state.

**Level 2 — Signed.** The response `TAI-Nonce` matches the
request nonce and the `TAI-Signature` verifies against a
key authorized for the retained service-domain and selector
under the policy selected before the request. Authorization
comes from authenticated DNS or independent configuration,
as described in (#verification). The reading is authenticated
and bound to that request. This does not establish clock
accuracy or bound network delay.

**Level 1 — Unique.** The response `TAI-Nonce` matches the
request nonce, but the signature is absent, the
`TAI-Key-Selector` is malformed, or no authorized key can
be obtained under the selected policy. The nonce echo
identifies the exchange, but does not authenticate the
responder or its claimed time. This includes inability
to authenticate DNS key data because its validation-time
prerequisites are not met. A mathematically valid signature
under an unauthenticated key does not raise this level.

**Level 0 — Plain.** The response carries no echoed
`TAI-Nonce`. The time value carries no freshness or
authentication guarantee from this protocol.

**Level -1 — Inconsistent.** Either the response
`TAI-Nonce` is present but does not match the request
nonce, or a key is authorized under the selected policy
but the `TAI-Signature` does not verify. The response
MUST be rejected.

A client MUST NOT use a response at trust level -1.
Whether a client accepts level 0 or level 1 for a purpose
not requiring authentication is an application policy
choice. It MUST NOT accept such a result when Level 2
is required. Clockless authenticated bootstrap requires
Level 2, not merely a matching nonce.

# Verification

This section applies when the response carries both
`TAI-Key-Selector` and `TAI-Signature`. Before making the
request, the client MUST retain the application-selected
service-domain and select its key-discovery policy.
Endpoint discovery MUST NOT change either. A verifier
reconstructs the framed payload as defined in (#signing).
The verifier MUST:

1. Confirm that the decoded `sf-binary` octets of the
   response `TAI-Nonce` match those of the request
   `TAI-Nonce`.
2. Confirm that the `TAI-Key-Selector` value is a
   well-formed selector matching the ABNF in (#signing).
3. Obtain a key authorized for the retained service-domain
   and selector under the preselected policy. In configured-key
   mode, use only the independently configured entry
   described in (#independently-configured-verification-keys),
   without a DNS key lookup. In DNS mode, resolve
   `<selector>._taistamp.<host>`, using the service-domain
   for `<host>`, parse the `v`, `k`, and `p` tags, and
   authenticate the complete key binding under (#dns-trust).
   Follow aliases under (#delegated-key-publication).
   Apply all validation-time requirements described in
   (#clockless-bootstrap-and-key-authentication); the
   candidate reading MUST NOT authenticate its own key.
4. Verify the Ed25519 signature over the framed payload
   using the authorized key selected in step 3.

A nonce mismatch in step 1 produces Inconsistent (level -1)
and MUST be rejected. A malformed selector in step 2, or
inability to obtain an authorized key in step 3, leaves a
nonce-matching response at Unique (level 1), subject to the
application's acceptance policy. A signature failure in
step 4 produces Inconsistent (level -1) and MUST be rejected.
Only successful completion of all four steps produces
Signed (level 2). A client requiring authenticated time
MUST fail the transaction when an authorized key cannot
be obtained; it MUST NOT use a Level 1 reading to satisfy
its authentication prerequisite.

Verifiers MUST use the strict verification procedure
described in Section 5.1.7 of [@!RFC8032].

# Security Considerations

## Threat Model

A Signed (level 2) Taistamp response authenticates a
single reading made in response to the client's request.
It does not establish a long-running synchronised clock
and does not bound network delay; a client is responsible for treating the
returned label as a measurement subject to the
round-trip time of its request. The trust level
framework (see (#trust-levels)) expresses the combined
freshness and authentication guarantees that a response
carries.

## Replay

A response is bound to its request only via the
`TAI-Nonce` echo and its inclusion in the signed
payload. A client that omits the nonce receives a
Plain (level 0) response with no freshness guarantee.
A client that reuses a nonce across requests gains no
replay protection even at level 1 or 2, since the nonce
no longer uniquely identifies the exchange. Clients
SHOULD generate a fresh, unpredictable nonce for each
request.

Replay protection is scoped to the client's own nonce
uniqueness enforcement. The protocol does not prevent
a recorded request/response pair from being presented
to a second application that does not track nonce
lifecycle. Clients SHOULD treat each Unique (level 1)
or Signed (level 2) response as valid for a single use
and SHOULD NOT accept a response whose nonce was issued
by a previous request.

At Level 1, the nonce echo is not authenticated. An
on-path attacker can observe the new nonce and attach
it to an arbitrary time value. Nonce matching alone
therefore MUST NOT be treated as authenticated freshness
or used to bootstrap key authentication.

## Domain Separation

The `taistamp-v1%x00` prefix prevents an Ed25519 key
configured for Taistamp from being induced to sign a
message valid under another protocol that happens to
embed the same suffix. Operators MUST NOT reuse a
Taistamp signing key for any other purpose.

## DNS Trust

For DNS-based key discovery, a Signed (level 2) result
depends on an authenticated binding from
`<selector>._taistamp.<host>` to the accepted public key.
Independently configured keys use the separate authorization
route in (#independently-configured-verification-keys).
An attacker able to replace unauthenticated
key-discovery data can substitute a public key and sign
an arbitrary time value with its corresponding private
key. A mathematically valid signature then does not
authenticate the intended service-domain. Without a
trusted key binding, an apparent Signed result has no
stronger service-identity guarantee than Unique (level 1).

Operators SHOULD publish key data under DNSSEC-signed
zones [@!RFC4033]. When using DNS to authorize a key,
a verifier MUST use DNSSEC-authenticated resolution or
an explicitly configured mechanism providing equivalent
key-binding guarantees, subject to the prerequisites in
(#clockless-bootstrap-and-key-authentication). An encrypted
connection to a resolver alone
does not authenticate unsigned authoritative DNS data.
A trusted validating resolver and a protected path to
its validation results can provide the required service.

Authentication MUST cover the complete binding from
the initial key owner to the accepted TXT data,
including aliases and the applicable authentication
chains [@!RFC4035]. Authenticating only a terminal TXT
RRset does not authenticate an unsigned alias pointing
to it. DNSSEC Bogus results MUST NOT be retried with
validation disabled to complete the same transaction.

The trust-level classifications in (#trust-levels) do
not override application acceptance policy. A required
authenticated Level 2 result MUST NOT be replaced by a
weaker accepted result after discovery or key-resolution
failure. Applications that permit lower levels retain
the policy choice described in (#trust-levels).

## Clockless Bootstrap and Key Authentication

Request freshness and key authentication have different
prerequisites. A fresh unpredictable nonce allows a
response to be bound to its request without a wall clock.
It does not supply the time needed to authenticate a
DNSSEC key binding or an X.509 certificate path.

Local DNSSEC validation requires a usable time estimate
for the RRSIG Inception and Expiration checks in Section
5.3.1 of [@!RFC4035]. Merely possessing a DNSSEC trust
anchor does not establish that an old signed key binding
is still authorized. Certificate-path validation similarly
checks validity at the validation time (Section 6.1.3 of
[@!RFC5280]). An arbitrary initial clock value or the
candidate Taistamp reading is not independent evidence
that these prerequisites are met.

A trusted validating resolver can use its own usable clock,
so the stub client need not perform those time checks
itself. However, the client MUST independently authenticate
its path to that resolver and trust its validation results.
Ordinary certificate-based channel authentication may
reintroduce a client-clock dependency. A securely configured
path whose authentication does not use the client's wall
clock can avoid that dependency. Encryption alone, or
an AD bit received through an unauthenticated path, is
insufficient. See Section 4.9.3 of [@!RFC4035].

A client with no usable wall clock MAY obtain Level 2 using
an independently configured verification key whose local
authorization is already established. Alternatively it MAY
use a trusted validation service through a clock-independent
authenticated path. Such clients MUST establish all key
and transport prerequisites before accepting the reading.
This document specifies configured service keys as one
route; it defines no new resolver authentication protocol.
Without an applicable route, a nonce-matching result is at
most Level 1, and authenticated bootstrap MUST fail.

The returned time MUST NOT be used as the sole basis for
accepting the validity periods of the DNSSEC chain or
certificate path needed to authorize that same reading.
Deferring those checks and testing them only against the
returned time demonstrates consistency, not current
key authorization. For example, an attacker with a retired
Taistamp private key and a recorded DNSSEC authentication
chain can sign the new request nonce with a claimed time
inside the common validity interval of that historical
chain. If its trust anchor is still accepted, deferred
checks can all pass even though the key is no longer
authorized. This is an authorization-replay attack, not
replay of an earlier nonce-bound Taistamp response.
This specification does not admit that procedure as an
independent basis for Level 2.

Configured service keys avoid this particular DNS dependency
but do not eliminate key compromise or revocation risks.
Authorization remains only as current as the client's
trusted configuration. A nonce cannot protect a client
that still authorizes a compromised key. See
(#independently-configured-verification-keys).

Configured Taistamp keys authenticate application responses,
not HTTPS certificates. Clockless operation MUST NOT bypass
an applicable HTTPS upgrade or certificate check. Direct
HTTP is usable only where explicitly permitted by the
application's transport policy; address discovery must
also be possible under that policy. Otherwise a separate
clock-independent transport arrangement or usable time
estimate is required.

A client using Taistamp to establish time MUST generate
fresh unpredictable nonces even immediately after reboot.
It MUST NOT rely on wall-clock values alone for nonce
uniqueness or unpredictability. A monotonic timer can measure
elapsed request time without knowing the calendar time,
but this protocol supplies no bound on network delay.
Applications requiring a bound SHOULD reject exchanges
whose measured elapsed time exceeds their configured
limit. A signature still attests only to the server's
claim, not the correctness of the server's clock.

An independently authenticated TAI reading may subsequently
be used as input to DNSSEC or certificate validity checks.
Clients doing so MUST account for measurement and delay
uncertainty and satisfy the trusted TAI-to-UTC conversion
requirements in (#leap-second-reporting). The server's
claimed leap-second count alone does not establish that
conversion. Obtaining time does not waive any remaining
validation or application-policy checks.

## Clock Manipulation

A signature attests that the server emitted a given
label; it does not attest that the server's clock is
correct. An operator whose clock is drifting or
deliberately set wrong will sign whatever label its
clock produces. Clients that depend on Taistamp for
high-stakes decisions SHOULD compare readings from
multiple independently operated services.

## Leap-Second Reporting

The `TAI-Leap-Seconds` header is included in the
signed payload as `leap-u32be`; the signature attests
that the server emitted this specific value, but not
that it is correct. Signing it allows the verifier to
reconstruct the server's own UTC interpretation of the
timestamp, even though the verifier need not trust that
value for UTC conversion purposes. An operator whose
leap-second table is wrong, or who deliberately sets it
wrong, will sign whatever count they emit. Clients who
require UTC equivalence MUST source the TAI-to-UTC
offset from a trusted table rather than from the
server.

## Key Revocation Window

Withdrawing a key TXT record or an authorizing selector
CNAME does not invalidate an already cached binding.
Until that binding expires under (#verifier-key-caching),
a verifier can still accept signatures from the compromised
private key. The relevant cache lifetime includes the
alias chain rather than only the terminal TXT record.

DNS TTLs bound ordinary cache retention, not every form
of revocation exposure. An attacker can replay an older
signed RRset while its RRSIG remains valid; a validating
resolver still applies the time and TTL checks in
Sections 5.3.1 and 5.3.3 of [@!RFC4035]. A short cache
TTL alone therefore does not guarantee that withdrawn
authorization cannot be accepted after that interval.

Operators SHOULD coordinate TTLs and signature-validity
periods for relevant key, alias, and delegation data.
DNS-only revocation is not instantaneous, and removing
an endpoint binding does not revoke a signing key.
These DNS lifetimes do not govern independently configured
keys, whose authorization must be withdrawn through the
client's trusted configuration process.

## Selector-Based DNS Amplification

In DNS mode, a verifier that performs one lookup per
incoming response carrying a selector that is malformed
or does not resolve amplifies query load onto the
authoritative server publishing the
`_taistamp.<host>` TXT records. Two cases arise: a cached key
that fails verification triggers a re-fetch per
(#verifier-key-caching); a previously unseen selector triggers
a first-time lookup, which the per-name re-fetch limit does not
bound. Verifiers MUST apply rate-limiting or exponential back-off
on all DNS lookups for names under `_taistamp.<host>`,
regardless of whether the lookup is a first fetch or a
re-fetch.

## Caching Intermediaries

Note: this section is non-normative.

Successful `GET` responses carry `Cache-Control: no-store`
(see (#successful-response)), which instructs intermediaries
not to retain or serve the response from cache. However,
CDN and proxy configurations sometimes override this
directive, for instance via path-based cache rules that
match `/.well-known/` resources. A client that included a
`TAI-Nonce` is protected: a cached response carries a
different nonce, producing Inconsistent (level -1), which
MUST be rejected. A client that did not include a `TAI-Nonce`
receives a Plain (level 0) response with no indication that
the time value is stale.

Operators deploying Taistamp behind a CDN or reverse
proxy SHOULD explicitly configure the intermediary to
pass `GET` responses to `/.well-known/taistamp` through
without caching, and SHOULD verify this behaviour
before relying on Signed (level 2) responses for
time-sensitive decisions.

## Delegation Security

Endpoint routing and signing-key authorization are
separate operations. A hostname CNAME or HTTPS service
binding changes connection establishment, not the
verification-key namespace. Only an authorized key binding
established through the
preselected DNS or configured-key mode authorizes a key
for the selected service-domain; see (#verification).

Clients MUST apply their configured destination-address
and port restrictions to discovered endpoints, including
addresses obtained after resolution. Discovery MUST NOT
bypass restrictions on local or private services. Clients
SHOULD omit unrelated ambient credentials when requesting
a timestamp. HTTPS discovery does not grant additional
HTTP authority to a provider beyond the original origin.

Independently trusted service-domains SHOULD use distinct
Taistamp signing keys. The v1 payload includes a selector
but no service-domain. If multiple domains authorize the
same public key under the same selector octets, a response
can verify for any of them when its other signed fields,
including the request nonce, match. Delegation does not
create cryptographic isolation between such domains.
This specification does not change the signed framing.

Delegation does not remove the general authentication
prerequisites in (#clockless-bootstrap-and-key-authentication).
Those prerequisites apply to direct and delegated endpoints
alike; this document defines no exception to them.

# IANA Considerations

## Well-Known URI

IANA is requested to register the following entry in
the "Well-Known URIs" registry [@!RFC8615]:

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| URI Suffix | `taistamp` |
| Change Controller | Apptly Software Ltd |
| Reference | This document |
| Status | permanent |
| Related Information | none |

## Media Type

IANA is requested to register the media type
`application/tai64n` in the "Media Types" registry
per [@?RFC6838]:

- Type name: `application`
- Subtype name: `tai64n`
- Required parameters: none
- Optional parameters: none
- Encoding considerations: 7-bit (ASCII)
- Security considerations: see (#security-considerations)
- Interoperability considerations: none
- Published specification: this document
- Applications that use this media type: time-
  authentication clients
- Fragment identifier considerations: none
- Restrictions on usage: none
- Provisional registration: no
- Author/Change controller: Apptly Software Ltd

## Header Fields

IANA is requested to register the following entries in
the "Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) Field Name
Registry" [@!RFC9110]:

| Field Name        | Status    | Structured Type | Reference     |
|-------------------|-----------|-----------------|---------------|
| `TAI-Leap-Seconds`| permanent | Integer         | this document |
| `TAI-Nonce`       | permanent | Binary          | this document |
| `TAI-Key-Selector`| permanent | Token           | this document |
| `TAI-Signature`   | permanent | Binary          | this document |

## Underscored DNS Node

Per [@!RFC8552], IANA is requested to add the following
entry to the "Underscored and Globally Scoped DNS Node
Names" registry:

| RR Type | _NODE NAME | Reference |
|---------|------------|-----------|
| TXT | `_taistamp` | (#key-publication) |

The HTTPS service binding uses the existing registrations
of [@!RFC9460]. This document requests no Taistamp SRV
service name, additional SVCB underscore entry, new
SvcParamKey, or new URI scheme.

